<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jawor</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jauer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jawor is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-silesia">Lower Silesia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. It was the capital of a Piast duchy from 1274, ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolko-i-the-strict">Bolko I the Strict</a> and his successors, and passed to the Bohemian crown in 1392. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>-framed Church of Peace, built by Lutherans in 1654–1655 under the terms of the Peace of Westphalia, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jawor.jpg" alt="Jawor" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fallaner · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jawor_aerial_view_2020_P17.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
